 (Welch's t-test) | **0.000063** |\n| **Cohen's d** | **−0.8367** |\n| **95% Bootstrap CI** (Lower, Upper) | **(−1.2489, −0.4710)** |\n| **Effect Size Interpretation** | **Large** |\n\n### Key Findings\n\n- **Group Statistics**: Alzheimers patients had a mean cognitive score of 85.27 (SD = 13.11), while Controls scored 96.62 (SD = 14.01).\n- **Statistical Significance**: The p-value (6.3 × 10⁻⁵) is well below the conventional α = 0.05 threshold, indicating a highly significant difference between groups.\n- **Effect Size**: Cohen's d = −0.84 (|d| ≥ 0.8) is classified as a **large** effect, meaning the difference is not only statistically significant but also practically meaningful.\n- **Bootstrap CI**: The 95% confidence interval (−1.25, −0.47) does not include zero, further confirming the robustness of the effect size estimate.\n- **Direction**: The negative Cohen's d reflects that Alzheimer's patients scored lower than controls, as expected biologically.\n\n**Output file**: `ttest_cohens_d_results.csv` — 1 row × 6 columns (Mean_Difference, P_Value, Cohens_d, CI_Lower, CI_Upper, Effect_Size_Interpretation)."], 'input_token_num': 32208, 'output_token_num': 7800}
